Step 1 – Create Amazon Cognito User Pool
1.1 What is a Cognito User Pool?
    An Amazon Cognito User Pool is a managed user directory and authentication service provided by AWS.
    
    Think of it as a centralized database of application users combined with an authentication engine.

    It manages:
    - User registration.
    - Username and password storage.
    - Password verification.
    - Password policies.
    - User account management.
    - Authentication and token generation.
    Instead of building our own authentication system using Lambda and DynamoDB, we can delegate these responsibilities to Cognito.
1.2 What are we going to create?
    We will create a User Pool named day2-auth-user-pool.
    Configuration	Value
    User Pool name	day2-auth-user-pool
    AWS Region	ap-south-1
    Sign-in method	Username
    Authentication	Username + Password
    MFA	Disabled
    Minimum password length	8

1.3 Create User Pool
Execute:
POOL_ID=$(aws cognito-idp create-user-pool \
  --pool-name day2-auth-user-pool \
  --username-configuration CaseSensitive=false \
  --mfa-configuration OFF \
  --policies 'PasswordPolicy={MinimumLength=8,RequireUppercase=true,RequireLowercase=true,RequireNumbers=true,RequireSymbols=true,TemporaryPasswordValidityDays=7}' \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --query 'UserPool.Id' \
  --output text)

  echo "Cognito User Pool ID: $POOL_ID"


    The command automatically captures the generated User Pool ID into the $POOL_ID variable.
1.4 Verify User Pool creation
    Execute:
    aws cognito-idp describe-user-pool \
      --user-pool-id "$POOL_ID" \
      --region ap-south-1 \
      --query 'UserPool.{Name:Name,Id:Id,MFA:MfaConfiguration,Status:Status}'
====================
Step 2 – Create Cognito App Client
2.1 What is an App Client?
    An App Client represents an application that is permitted to communicate with a Cognito User Pool.
    
    Think of it as an identity for your application.
    
    In our case:
    - Cognito User Pool manages users and passwords.
    - App Client allows our  application to initiate authentication.
    - Cognito verifies the credentials and returns authentication tokens.
    Our frontend will use the App Client ID to identify itself when communicating with Cognito.
2.2 What are we going to create?
    Configuration	Value
    App Client name	day2-auth-web-client
    Authentication	Username + Password
    Client secret	Disabled
    Region	ap-south-1

Why no client secret?

    Our application runs inside a browser. Any secret embedded in JavaScript or HTML can be viewed by users. 
    Therefore, we must use a public client without a client secret.
2.3 Create App Client
Execute:
    CLIENT_ID=$(aws cognito-idp create-user-pool-client \
      --user-pool-id "$POOL_ID" \
      --client-name day2-auth-web-client \
      --no-generate-secret \
      --explicit-auth-flows ALLOW_USER_PASSWORD_AUTH ALLOW_REFRESH_TOKEN_AUTH \
      --prevent-user-existence-errors ENABLED \
      --region ap-south-1 \
      --query 'UserPoolClient.ClientId' \
      --output text)
    
    echo "Cognito App Client ID: $CLIENT_ID"


This automatically saves the App Client ID in $CLIENT_ID.
2.4 Verify App Client
Execute:
aws cognito-idp describe-user-pool-client \
  --user-pool-id "$POOL_ID" \
  --client-id "$CLIENT_ID" \
  --region ap-south-1 \
  --query 'UserPoolClient.{ClientName:ClientName,ClientId:ClientId,HasSecret:GenerateSecret,AuthFlows:ExplicitAuthFlows}'

